## Concept tags

Concept tag: apply only when dominant, i.e. the problem cannot be solved on the intended efficient path without it, or a distractor targets it. Never apply because the stem merely mentions it.

- c.force.norm: Apply when the normal reaction is the crux: its value depends on the motion (lift, vertical circle, accelerated contact); the loss-of-contact condition N = 0; its direction at a curved surface, edge or corner; or the normal from the part of a body in contact. Do not apply when N = mg cos(theta) or N = mg is a routine step, or when only the friction regime is tested (c.force.static-kinetic).
- c.force.static-kinetic: Apply when the friction regime is the crux: static or kinetic, required versus limiting friction, the onset of slipping, angle of repose, minimum force to start or prevent motion. Typical distractor: using mu*N where the required static friction is smaller. Do not apply when the regime is stated (the block is sliding) and mu_k*N is simply substituted.
- c.force.friction-direction: Apply when the direction of friction must be reasoned from relative motion or its tendency: stacked blocks, walking or running, rolling wheels, belts and conveyors, a block on an accelerating truck. Do not apply when the direction is obvious, such as opposing a block's slide on a fixed floor.
- c.force.transmission: Apply when the force a connector carries is non-trivial: pulley force multiplication, the force on a pulley axle or clamp (2T), tension varying along a heavy string, tension stepping along a light rope at points of applied load, a self-propelled body (monkey climbing a rope) setting the tension. Typical distractors: forgetting the factor 2T, or assuming one uniform tension throughout. Do not apply for uniform tension in a single light string over an ideal pulley; that is primitive.
- c.dyn.equilibrium: Apply when the crux is equilibrium of concurrent, non-collinear forces needing resolution or Lami's theorem: a weight held by strings at angles, a body on an incline held by an applied force, a hanging sign. Do not apply to collinear balance (T = mg) or to trivial vertical balance in a dynamics problem.
- c.dyn.sys-choice: Apply when choosing the system boundary is the crux, in one of three forms: (1) take the whole system so internal forces drop out (chain over a pulley); (2) section a body or connector to extract an internal force (tension at a point in a rope); (3) replace a subsystem by an equivalent body (equivalent mass of a movable pulley with two hanging blocks). Do not apply when straightforward per-body free-body diagrams solve the problem directly.
- c.dyn.constraint-rel: Apply when relating accelerations or velocities through a constraint is non-trivial: string-length relations with movable pulleys or several strings, the common-normal condition between a block and a movable wedge, rigid-link relations. Do not apply for a single inextensible string over a fixed pulley (equal and opposite motion is primitive), or when an equivalent-body reduction already absorbs the constraint.
- c.dyn.non-inertial-frame: Apply when analysing the motion in an accelerating or rotating frame with a pseudo force is the efficient path, or a distractor targets it: block or pendulum in an accelerating lift or car, block on an accelerating wedge solved in the wedge frame, apparent weight, centrifugal force in a rotating frame. Do not apply merely because an accelerating platform is present when the ground-frame solution is just as direct.
- c.dyn.inst-change: Apply when the question asks for the state just after a cut, release or sudden change: spring forces persist (the extension cannot change instantly) while string tensions and normal reactions jump. Typical case: accelerations of blocks immediately after a string in a spring-block system is cut. Do not apply to ordinary steady or continuous dynamics.
- c.circular.horz: Apply when the crux is horizontal circular motion with net radial force mv^2/r and vertical balance: conical pendulum, banked road speed limits, well of death, a block on a rotating platform held by friction, a bead on a rotating rod. Do not apply to vertical circles (c.circular.vert).
- c.circular.vert: Apply when the crux is motion in a vertical circle: the minimum speed at the top by contact type (string or one-sided track: tension or normal >= 0; rod or two-sided path: speed >= 0), tension or normal at the top, bottom or a general angle, or where the body leaves the circle. Typical distractor: swapping the string and rod conditions. Energy conservation used alongside does not earn a separate tag here.
- c.momentum.rate: Apply when F = dp/dt is the crux: reading force as the slope of a p-t graph, thrust from mass flow (rocket, conveyor, water jet), force from a stream of bullets or particles per second. Do not apply to single-impact impulse questions (c.momentum.impulse).
- c.momentum.impulse: Apply when J = integral of F dt = change in momentum is the crux: average or impulsive force in a short contact, the area under an F-t graph, catching or hitting, impulsive tension when a slack string becomes taut. Do not apply to continuous mass-flow forces (c.momentum.rate).
- c.momentum.sys: Apply when the crux is system momentum: conservation when the net external force is zero (fully or along one direction), or centre-of-mass motion M*a_cm = F_ext: man walking on a boat or plank, explosion, bullet embedding in a block, recoil. Do not apply when momentum of a single body is used only through F = dp/dt or impulse.

## Math tags

Math-technique tag: apply only when the technique is the crux of the intended solution or a distractor targets it; routine steps are never tagged.

- m.differentiation: Apply when differentiation drives the solution: velocity or acceleration from a position function, an extremum found by setting a derivative to zero, a rate of change. Do not apply to reading a standard derivative result already known as a formula.
- m.integration: Apply when integration drives the solution: impulse from F(t), motion under a variable force, accumulation under variable mass, work by a variable force. Do not apply when the integral is the area of a simple shape on a graph (m.graphical-analysis).
- m.diff-eqn: Apply when the solution needs setting up and solving a differential equation: velocity-dependent drag, a chain sliding off a table, the rocket equation, separable forms. Do not apply when a direct integration of a known function suffices (m.integration).
- m.vectors: Apply when vector algebra is the crux: resolution in three dimensions or along non-obvious axes, dot or cross products, relative-velocity or relative-acceleration vector triangles. Do not apply to routine resolution along and normal to an incline.
- m.plane-geometry: Apply when plane geometry is the crux: angle in a semicircle, chords of a circle (time of descent along chords), similar triangles in string or rod configurations, the geometry of contact points. Do not apply to simple right-triangle resolution.
- m.3d-geometry: Apply when the solution needs lines, planes or distances in three dimensions. Do not apply to planar problems drawn in perspective.
- m.conics: Apply when a locus or path is a parabola, ellipse or circle and its properties drive the solution: a body on a parabolic wire, a trajectory or locus identified as a conic. Do not apply to ordinary circular motion where only the radius is used.
- m.trigonometry: Apply when trigonometric identities or optimisation over an angle are the crux: the angle for minimum pulling force (tan(theta) = mu), maximising a*cos(theta) + b*sin(theta) to sqrt(a^2 + b^2), angle-dependent extrema. Do not apply to substituting standard sine and cosine values.
- m.simultaneous-eqn: Apply when the intended path requires eliminating three or more unknowns, or a non-obvious elimination. Do not apply to two-unknown routine solving, or when the intended path avoids the system altogether (for example by a system choice).
- m.quadratic: Apply when a quadratic drives the solution: solving for two times or positions, nature of roots or discriminant conditions (whether an event happens), the extremum of a quadratic expression. Do not apply to a quadratic that factorises trivially as an incidental step.
- m.graphical-analysis: Apply when reading a graph is the crux: slope, area or intercepts of F-t, p-t, v-t, a-t or a-x graphs, or matching a situation to the correct graph. Do not apply when a graph merely illustrates data that are also given numerically.

## Dependencies

Dependency: list another topic only when it is critical to solving the problem, at most two. These uses are routine in Laws of Motion problems and never make a dependency on their own:

- Kinematics (103): the constant-acceleration equations used to finish once the acceleration is found (distance, time or speed after the dynamics is solved). It is a dependency only when the motion analysis itself is the crux, such as relative motion between two accelerating bodies or a projectile after a body leaves a surface.
- Work, Energy and Power (105): energy conservation used alongside a vertical circle to get the speed at a point (c.circular.vert covers it), or a work-energy step that only finishes a force problem. It is a dependency only when work, energy or power is the intended path, such as the work done by friction along a path or the power a force delivers.
- Rotational Motion (106): a pulley or wheel taken as massless and ideal, which is not rotational dynamics at all. It is a dependency only when moment of inertia, torque or rolling is part of the solution.
